 sin⁻¹(sin(-π/3)) = -π/3.                                             [1 mark]\n269: \n270:     (iii) tan⁻¹(tan(5π/6)):\n271:         Since 5π/6 ∉ (-π/2, π/2) (the principal value branch of tan⁻¹),\n272:         tan(5π/6) = tan(π - π/6) = -tan(π/6) = -1/√3.\n273:         tan⁻¹(-1/√3) = -tan⁻¹(1/√3) = -π/6.                                  [1 mark]\n274: \n275:     Final Answers: (i) 3π/4, (ii) -π/3, (iii) -π/6\n276: \n277: Q27. Solution:\n278:     (i) Domain of f(x) = sin⁻¹(3x - 1):\n279:         For sin⁻¹(u), domain is -1 ≤ u ≤ 1.\n280:         -1 ≤ 3x - 1 ≤ 1\n281:         Adding 1: 0 ≤ 3x ≤ 2\n282:         Dividing by 3: 0 ≤ x ≤ 2/3\n283:         Domain = [0, 2/3].                                                   [1 mark]\n284: \n285:     (ii) Domain of g(x) = cos⁻¹((x - 1)/2):\n286:         For cos⁻¹(u), domain is -1 ≤ u ≤ 1.\n287:         -1 ≤ (x - 1)/2 ≤ 1\n288:         Multiplying by 2: -2 ≤ x - 1 ≤ 2\n289:         Adding 1: -1 ≤ x ≤ 3\n290:         Domain = [-1, 3].                                                    [1 mark]\n291: \n292:     (iii) Principal value of tan⁻¹(-1):\n293:         Let θ = tan⁻¹(-1).\n294:         tan θ = -1 = -tan(π/4) = tan(-π/4).\n295:         Since -π/4 ∈ (-π/2, π/2),\n296:         tan⁻¹(-1) = -π/4.                                                    [1 mark]\n297: \n298:     Final Answers: (i) [0, 2/3], (ii) [-1, 3], (iii) -π/4\n299: \n300: Q28. Solution:\n301:     Given determinant:\n302:         | 2  -1   3 |\n303:     Δ = | 1   2  -1 |\n304:         | 0   3   2 |\n305: \n306:     Expanding along the first row (R1):\n307:     Δ = 2 · | 2  -1 | - (-1) · | 1  -1 | + 3 · | 1   2 |\n308:             | 3   2 |          | 0   2 |       | 0   3 |                     [1 mark]\n309: \n310:     Evaluating the 2x2 determinants:\n311:     | 2  -1 | = (2)(2) - (-1)(3) = 4 + 3 = 7\n312:     | 3   2 |\n313: \n314:     | 1  -1 | = (1)(2) - (-1)(0) = 2 - 0 = 2\n315:     | 0   2 |\n316: \n317:     | 1   2 | = (1)(3) - (2)(0) = 3 - 0 = 3\n318:     | 0   3 |                                                                [1 mark]\n319: \n320:     Substituting values:\n321:     Δ = 2(7) + 1(2) + 3(3)\n322:       = 14 + 2 + 9\n323:       = 25                                                                   [1 mark]\n324: \n325:     Final Answer: 25\n326: \n327: Q29. Solution:\n328:     Given: y = x^(sin x), where x > 0\n329: \n330:     Step 1: Taking natural logarithm on both sides:\n331:     ln y = ln(x^(sin x))\n332:     ln y = sin x · ln x                                                      [½ mark]\n333: \n334:     Step 2: Differentiating both sides with respect to x:\n335:     (1/y) · (dy/dx) = d/dx [sin x · ln x]                                    [½ mark]\n336: \n337:     Using the product rule on the right hand side:\n338:     (1/y) · (dy/dx) = (d/dx(sin x)) · ln x + sin x · (d/dx(ln x))\n339:     (1/y) · (dy/dx) = cos x · ln x + sin x · (1/x)                           [1 mark]\n340: \n341:     Step 3: Multiplying by y:\n342:     dy/dx = y [cos x · ln x + (sin x)/x]\n343:     Substituting y = x^(sin x):\n344:     dy/dx = x^(sin x) [cos x · ln x + (sin x)/x]                             [1 mark]\n345: \n346:     Final Answer: dy/dx = x^(sin x) [cos x · ln x + (sin x)/x]\n347: \n348: Q30. Solution:\n349:     Given function: f(x) = x³ - 6x² + 9x + 15\n350: \n351:     Step 1: Differentiate f(x) with respect to x:\n352:     f'(x) = 3x² - 12x + 9                                                    [½ mark]\n353:     f'(x) = 3(x² - 4x + 3) = 3(x - 1)(x - 3)                                 [½ mark]\n354: \n355:     Step 2: Find critical points:\n356:     f'(x) = 0 ⟹ 3(x - 1)(x - 3) = 0 ⟹ x = 1, x = 3.\n357:     The points x = 1 and x = 3 divide R into three intervals:\n358:     (-∞, 1), (1, 3), (3, ∞).\n359: \n360:     Step 3: Determine the sign of f'(x) in each interval:\n361:     - In (-∞, 1): For x = 0, f'(0) = 3(-1)(-3) = 9 > 0 ⟹ f'(x) > 0.\n362:     - In (1, 3): For x = 2, f'(2) = 3(1)(-1) = -3 < 0 ⟹ f'(x) < 0.\n363:     - In (3, ∞): For x = 4, f'(4) = 3(3)(1) = 9 > 0 ⟹ f'(x) > 0.             [1 mark]\n364: \n365:     Conclusions:\n366:     (i) f(x) is strictly increasing in (-∞, 1) ∪ (3, ∞).                     [¾ mark]\n367:     (ii) f(x) is strictly decreasing in (1, 3).                              [¾ mark]\n368: \n369:     Final Answers: (i) Strictly increasing in (-∞, 1) ∪ (3, ∞), (ii) Strictly decreasing in (1, 3)\n370: \n371: Q31. Solution:\n372:     Evaluate: I = ∫ x · cos x dx\n373: \n374:     Using Integration by Parts:\n375:     ∫ u · v dx = u ∫ v dx - ∫ [u' · (∫ v dx)] dx\n376: \n377:     By ILATE rule:\n378:     Let u = x (algebraic function) ⟹ du/dx = 1 ⟹ du = dx                  [½ mark]\n379:     Let dv = cos x dx (trigonometric function) ⟹ v = ∫ cos x dx = sin x     [½ mark]\n380: \n381:     Applying the formula:\n382:     I = x · sin x - ∫ 1 · sin x dx                                           [1 mark]\n383:     I = x sin x - (-cos x) + C\n384:     I = x sin x + cos x + C                                                  [1 mark]\n385: \n386:     Final Answer: x sin x + cos x + C\n387: \n388: ================================================================================\n389:                                    SECTION D\n390:                          (4 Questions x 5 Marks = 20 Marks)\n391: ================================================================================\n392: \n393: Q32. Solution:\n394:     Given matrix:\n395:         |  3  -2   5 |\n396:     A = |  4   1  -3 |\n397:         | -1   2   1 |\n398: \n399:     Step 1: Find the transpose of A, Aᵀ:\n400:          |  3   4  -1 |\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
