{"step_index":18,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T15:52:56Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-24T21:22:56+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-24T21:22:56+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/rajan/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/math_exam_2026/Set_B_Marking_Scheme.txt`\nTotal Lines: 624\nTotal Bytes: 26270\nShowing lines 401 to 624\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n401:     Aᵀ = | -2   1   2 |\n402:          |  5  -3   1 |                                                      [1 mark]\n403: \n404:     Step 2: Find symmetric matrix P = (1/2)(A + Aᵀ):\n405:              | 3+3  -2+4   5-1 |     | 6   2   4 |\n406:     A + Aᵀ = | 4-2   1+1  -3+2 |  =  | 2   2  -1 |\n407:              |-1+5   2-3   1+1 |     | 4  -1   2 |\n408: \n409:         | 3    1    2   |\n410:     P = | 1    1   -1/2 |                                                    [1½ marks]\n411:         | 2   -1/2  1   |\n412: \n413:     Verification of symmetry:\n414:          | 3    1    2   |\n415:     Pᵀ = | 1    1   -1/2 | = P. Hence P is symmetric.                        [½ mark]\n416:          | 2   -1/2  1   |\n417: \n418:     Step 3: Find skew-symmetric matrix Q = (1/2)(A - Aᵀ):\n419:              | 3-3   -2-4   5-(-1) |     |  0  -6   6 |\n420:     A - Aᵀ = | 4-(-2) 1-1  -3-2    |  =  |  6   0  -5 |\n421:              |-1-5    2-(-3) 1-1   |     | -6   5   0 |\n422: \n423:         |  0   -3    3   |\n424:     Q = |  3    0   -5/2 |                                                   [1 mark]\n425:         | -3    5/2  0   |\n426: \n427:     Verification of skew-symmetry:\n428:          |  0    3   -3   |\n429:     Qᵀ = | -3    0    5/2 | = -Q. Hence Q is skew-symmetric.                 [½ mark]\n430:          |  3   -5/2  0   |\n431: \n432:     Step 4: Verify P + Q = A:\n433:             | 3+0    1-3    2+3       |   |  3  -2   5 |\n434:     P + Q = | 1+3    1+0   -1/2-5/2   | = |  4   1  -3 | = A                 [½ mark]\n435:             | 2-3   -1/2+5/2 1+0      |   | -1   2   1 |\n436: \n437:     Final Answer: A = P + Q, where\n438:     P = [[3, 1, 2], [1, 1, -1/2], [2, -1/2, 1]] (Symmetric matrix)\n439:     Q = [[0, -3, 3], [3, 0, -5/2], [-3, 5/2, 0]] (Skew-symmetric matrix)\n440: \n441: Q33. Solution:\n442:     Given system of linear equations:\n443:      x -  y + 2z = 7\n444:     3x + 4y - 5z = -5\n445:     2x -  y + 3z = 12\n446: \n447:     Writing the system in matrix form AX = B:\n448:         | 1  -1   2 |        | x |        |  7 |\n449:     A = | 3   4  -5 |,   X = | y |,   B = | -5 |                             [½ mark]\n450:         | 2  -1   3 |        | z |        | 12 |\n451: \n452:     Step 1: Calculate determinant |A|:\n453:     |A| = 1 · (4·3 - (-5)(-1)) - (-1) · (3·3 - (-5)(2)) + 2 · (3(-1) - 4·2)\n454:         = 1 · (12 - 5) + 1 · (9 + 10) + 2 · (-3 - 8)\n455:         = 1(7) + 1(19) + 2(-11)\n456:         = 7 + 19 - 22 = 4 ≠ 0\n457:     Since |A| ≠ 0, A⁻¹ exists and the system has a unique solution X = A⁻¹B. [1 mark]\n458: \n459:     Step 2: Calculate cofactors of elements of matrix A:\n460:     C₁₁ = +[(4)(3) - (-5)(-1)] = 12 - 5 = 7\n461:     C₁₂ = -[(3)(3) - (-5)(2)] = -(9 + 10) = -19\n462:     C₁₃ = +[(3)(-1) - (4)(2)] = -3 - 8 = -11\n463:     C₂₁ = -[(-1)(3) - (2)(-1)] = -(-3 + 2) = 1\n464:     C₂₂ = +[(1)(3) - (2)(2)] = 3 - 4 = -1\n465:     C₂₃ = -[(1)(-1) - (-1)(2)] = -(-1 + 2) = -1\n466:     C₃₁ = +[(-1)(-5) - (2)(4)] = 5 - 8 = -3\n467:     C₃₂ = -[(1)(-5) - (2)(3)] = -(-5 - 6) = 11\n468:     C₃₃ = +[(1)(4) - (-1)(3)] = 4 + 3 = 7                                    [1½ marks]\n469: \n470:     Step 3: Adjoint of matrix A:\n471:              | C₁₁  C₂₁  C₃₁ |     |   7   1  -3 |\n472:     adj(A) = | C₁₂  C₂₂  C₃₂ |  =  | -19  -1  11 |                           [½ mark]\n473:              | C₁₃  C₂₃  C₃₃ |     | -11  -1   7 |\n474: \n475:     Step 4: Inverse matrix A⁻¹:\n476:     A⁻¹ = (1/|A|) adj(A) = (1/4) [[7, 1, -3], [-19, -1, 11], [-11, -1, 7]]    [½ mark]\n477: \n478:     Step 5: Compute X = A⁻¹B:\n479:     | x |         |   7   1  -3 | |  7 |\n480:     | y | = (1/4) | -19  -1  11 | | -5 |\n481:     | z |         | -11  -1   7 | | 12 |\n482: \n483:     x = (1/4) [7(7) + 1(-5) + (-3)(12)]    = (1/4) [49 - 5 - 36]   = (1/4)(8)  = 2\n484:     y = (1/4) [-19(7) + (-1)(-5) + 11(12)] = (1/4) [-133 + 5 + 132] = (1/4)(4) = 1\n485:     z = (1/4) [-11(7) + (-1)(-5) + 7(12)]  = (1/4) [-77 + 5 + 84]  = (1/4)(12) = 3   [1 mark]\n486: \n487:     Final Answer: x = 2, y = 1, z = 3\n488: \n489: Q34. Solution:\n490:     Given: y = (tan⁻¹x)²\n491: \n492:     Step 1: First differentiation:\n493:     Differentiating both sides with respect to x:\n494:     y₁ = dy/dx = 2(tan⁻¹x) · d/dx(tan⁻¹x)                                     [½ mark]\n495:     y₁ = 2(tan⁻¹x) · [1 / (1 + x²)]                                          [½ mark]\n496: \n497:     Step 2: Cross multiplying to avoid quotient rule:\n498:     (1 + x²) y₁ = 2 tan⁻¹x  ... (1)                                          [1 mark]\n499: \n500:     Step 3: Second differentiation:\n501:     Differentiating equation (1) with respect to x using product rule on LHS:\n502:     d/dx [(1 + x²) y₁] = d/dx [2 tan⁻¹x]                                     [½ mark]\n503:     (1 + x²) · (dy₁/dx) + y₁ · d/dx(1 + x²) = 2 · [1 / (1 + x²)]              [½ mark]\n504:     (1 + x²) y₂ + 2x y₁ = 2 / (1 + x²)                                       [1 mark]\n505: \n506:     Step 4: Multiplying the entire equation by (1 + x²):\n507:     (1 + x²)² y₂ + 2x(1 + x²) y₁ = 2                                         [½ mark]\n508: \n509:     Since y₁ = dy/dx and y₂ = d²y/dx²:\n510:     (1 + x²)² · (d²y/dx²) + 2x(1 + x²) · (dy/dx) = 2\n511: \n512:     Hence Proved.                                                            [½ mark]\n513: \n514: Q35. Solution:\n515:     Evaluate: I = ∫ (x² + x + 1) / ((x + 1)(x + 2)) dx\n516: \n517:     Step 1: Expand denominator:\n518:     (x + 1)(x + 2) = x² + 3x + 2\n519: \n520:     Step 2: Perform polynomial division (as degree of numerator = degree of denominator = 2):\n521:     (x² + x + 1) / (x² + 3x + 2) = [(x² + 3x + 2) - 2x - 1] / (x² + 3x + 2)\n522:                                  = 1 + (-2x - 1) / ((x + 1)(x + 2))          [1 mark]\n523: \n524:     Step 3: Partial fraction decomposition of (-2x - 1) / ((x + 1)(x + 2)):\n525:     Let (-2x - 1) / ((x + 1)(x + 2)) = A / (x + 1) + B / (x + 2)             [½ mark]\n526:     ⟹ -2x - 1 = A(x + 2) + B(x + 1)\n527: \n528:     Substituting x = -1:\n529:     -2(-1) - 1 = A(-1 + 2) + B(0)\n530:     2 - 1 = A(1) ⟹ A = 1                                                    [½ mark]\n531: \n532:     Substituting x = -2:\n533:     -2(-2) - 1 = A(0) + B(-2 + 1)\n534:     4 - 1 = B(-1) ⟹ 3 = -B ⟹ B = -3                                         [½ mark]\n535: \n536:     Therefore:\n537:     (x² + x + 1) / ((x + 1)(x + 2)) = 1 + 1/(x + 1) - 3/(x + 2)              [½ mark]\n538: \n539:     Step 4: Integrating term by term:\n540:     I = ∫ [1 + 1/(x + 1) - 3/(x + 2)] dx                                     [½ mark]\n541:       = ∫ 1 dx + ∫ 1/(x + 1) dx - 3 ∫ 1/(x + 2) dx\n542:       = x + ln|x + 1| - 3 ln|x + 2| + C                                      [1 mark]\n543: \n544:     Final Answer: x + ln|x + 1| - 3 ln|x + 2| + C\n545:     (or x + ln |(x + 1)/(x + 2)³| + C)\n546: \n547: ================================================================================\n548:                                    SECTION E\n549:             (3 Case Study Questions x 4 Marks = 12 Marks)\n550: ================================================================================\n551: \n552: Q36. Case Study - Relations and Functions\n553:     Given set A = {1, 2, 3, 4, 6} and relation R = {(a, b) : a divides b, a, b ∈ A}.\n554: \n555:     (i) Show that R is reflexive and transitive:                             (2 marks)\n556:         - Reflexivity:\n557:           For any a ∈ A, a divides a (since a/a = 1, an integer).\n558:           Thus, (a, a) ∈ R for all a ∈ A.\n559:           Hence, R is reflexive.                                             [1 mark]\n560: \n561:         - Transitivity:\n562:           Let (a, b) ∈ R and (b, c) ∈ R for a, b, c ∈ A.\n563:           (a, b) ∈ R ⟹ a divides b ⟹ b = k₁a for some integer k₁.\n564:           (b, c) ∈ R ⟹ b divides c ⟹ c = k₂b for some integer k₂.\n565:           Substituting b: c = k₂(k₁a) = (k₁k₂)a.\n566:           Since k₁k₂ is an integer, a divides c.\n567:           Thus, (a, c) ∈ R.\n568:           Hence, R is transitive.                                            [1 mark]\n569: \n570:     (ii) Is R symmetric? Justify with an example:                            (1 mark)\n571:         No, R is not symmetric.                                              [½ mark]\n572:         Counter-example:\n573:         (1, 2) ∈ R because 1 divides 2, but (2, 1) ∉ R because 2 does not divide 1 (1/2 is not an integer).  [½ mark]\n574: \n575:     (iii) Is R an equivalence relation? Give reason:                         (1 mark)\n576:         No, R is not an equivalence relation.                                [½ mark]\n577:         Reason: A relation is an equivalence relation if and only if it is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive. Since R is not symmetric, it cannot be an equivalence relation.  [½ mark]\n578: \n579: Q37. Case Study - Application of Derivatives\n580:     Given total revenue function: R(x) = 13x² + 26x + 15 (in ₹).\n581: \n582:     (i) Find the marginal revenue function, MR(x) = dR/dx:                   (2 marks)\n583:         MR(x) = d/dx [R(x)]                                                  [½ mark]\n584:         MR(x) = d/dx [13x² + 26x + 15]\n585:         MR(x) = 13(2x) + 26(1) + 0                                           [1 mark]\n586:         MR(x) = 26x + 26                                                     [½ mark]\n587: \n588:     (ii) Find the marginal revenue when x = 7 units:                         (1 mark)\n589:         MR(7) = 26(7) + 26                                                   [½ mark]\n590:         MR(7) = 182 + 26 = ₹ 208.                                            [½ mark]\n591: \n592:     (iii) State with reason whether revenue is increasing or decreasing at x = 7: (1 mark)\n593:         Revenue is strictly increasing at x = 7.                             [½ mark]\n594:         Reason: At x = 7, dR/dx = MR(7) = 208 > 0. Since the derivative (rate of change) of the revenue function is positive, total revenue is increasing at x = 7.  [½ mark]\n595: \n596: Q38. Case Study - Integrals\n597:     Given velocity v(t) = 6t² - 4t (in m/s), with initial condition s(0) = 0 at t = 0.\n598: \n599:     (i) Find the displacement function s(t):                                 (2 marks)\n600:         We know that v(t) = ds/dt ⟹ s(t) = ∫ v(t) dt                         [½ mark]\n601:         s(t) = ∫ (6t² - 4t) dt\n602:         s(t) = 6(t³/3) - 4(t²/2) + C\n603:         s(t) = 2t³ - 2t² + C                                                 [1 mark]\n604: \n605:         Using initial condition s(0) = 0:\n606:         2(0)³ - 2(0)² + C = 0 ⟹ C = 0.\n607:         Therefore, displacement function is s(t) = 2t³ - 2t².                [½ mark]\n608: \n609:     (ii) Find the displacement at t = 3 s:                                   (1 mark)\n610:         s(3) = 2(3)³ - 2(3)²                                                 [½ mark]\n611:         s(3) = 2(27) - 2(9) = 54 - 18 = 36 m.                                [½ mark]\n612:         Displacement at t = 3 s is 36 metres.\n613: \n614:     (iii) Find the value of t (t > 0) at which the car is momentarily at rest: (1 mark)\n615:         The car is momentarily at rest when its velocity v(t) = 0.           [½ mark]\n616:         6t² - 4t = 0\n617:         2t(3t - 2) = 0\n618:         ⟹ t = 0  or  3t - 2 = 0 ⟹ t = 2/3 s.\n619:         Since t > 0, t = 2/3 s (or ~0.67 s).                                 [½ mark]\n620: \n621: ================================================================================\n622:                          -- End of Marking Scheme --\n623: ================================================================================\n624: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
